          %meta{:content => "application/xhtml+xml;charset=utf-8", "http-equiv" => "Content-type"}/
          %title Pre-Lie algebras
          %h1#prelie_algebras Pre-Lie algebras
          .maruku_toc
            %ul
              %li
                %a{:href => "#definition"} Definition
              %li
                %a{:href => "#examples"} Examples
              %li
                %a{:href => "#relation_to_the_work_of_connes_and_kreimer"} Relation to the work of Connes and Kreimer
              %li
                %a{:href => "#selfreferentiality"} Self-referentiality
              %li
                %a{:href => "#references"} References
          %h2#definition Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong pre-Lie algebra
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+space"} vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with a bilinear operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot: A \times A \to A
            such that
          #eq:basic_identity.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %mi a
                      %mo ,
                      %mi b
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_a, L_b] = L_{[a,b]}
          %p
            for all
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                    %mo ,
                    %mi b
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a, b\in A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_a
            is the operation of left multiplication by
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi a
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mi b
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} L_a b = a \cdot b
          %p
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_a, L_b]
            is the usual commutator of operators using
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/composition"} composition
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %mo −
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi b
                  %msub
                    %mi L
                    %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [L_a, L_b] = L_a L_b - L_b L_a
          %p
            while
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b]
            is the commutator defined using the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot
            operation:
          #eq:torsion_free_identity.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (2)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                  %mo −
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b] = a \cdot b - b \cdot a
          %p
            Unravelling this, we see a pre-Lie algebra is vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            equipped with a bilinear operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo ×
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \cdot: A \times A \to A
            such that
          #eq:identity.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (3)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo −
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi c
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi c
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  a \cdot (b \cdot c) - b \cdot (a \cdot c) =
                  (a \cdot b) \cdot c - (b \cdot a) \cdot c
          %p
            More precisely, this is a
            %strong left
            pre-Lie algebra. We can also define right pre-Lie algebras.
          %p
            Every
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/associative+algebra"} associative algebra
            is a pre-Lie algebra, but not conversely. The reason pre-Lie algebras have the name they do is that this weakening of the concept of associative algebra is still enough to give a
            = succeed "!" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lie+algebra"} Lie algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is an associative algebra, the operation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %mi a
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi b
                  %mo −
                  %mi b
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [a,b] = a \cdot b - b \cdot a
          %p
            makes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            into a Lie algebra. But this is also true for pre-Lie algebras! It is a fun exercise to derive the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Jacobi+identity"} Jacobi identity
            from equation
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:identity"} (3)
          %ul
            %li Chengming Bai, An introduction to pre-Lie algebras. In: Algebra and Applications 1: Nonssociative Algebras and Categories, Wiley Online Library (2021), 245-273.
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            First, given a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            with a flat torsion-free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection"} connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            on its
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+bundle"} tangent bundle
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tangent+vector+fields"} tangent vector fields
            into a pre-Lie algebra by defining
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi v
                  %mo ⋅
                  %mi w
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∇
                    %mi v
                  %mi w
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} v \cdot w = \nabla_v w
          %p
            The definition of ‘flat’ is precisely
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:basic_identity"} (1)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:torsion_free_identity"} (2)
          %p
            Second, suppose
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi O
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operad"} linear operad
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            be the free
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebra+of+an+operad"} algebra
            on one generator. As a vector space we have
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A = \bigoplus_{n} O_n/S_n \, .
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            is the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi O
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O_n
            of
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            becomes a pre-Lie algebra in a manner described here:
          %ul
            %li
              Dominique Manchon,
              %a{:href => "http://math.univ-bpclermont.fr/~manchon/biblio/ESI-prelie2009.pdf"} A short survey on pre-Lie algebras
          %p
            Third, the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hochschild+cohomology"} Hochschild
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complex"} chain complex
            of any associative algebra, with grading shifted down by one, can be given the structure of a ‘
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+object"} graded
            pre-Lie algebra’, as discovered by Gerstenhaber and described here:
          %ul
            %li
              Justin Thomas,
              %a{:href => "http://www.math.northwestern.edu/~jdthomas/Talk%20Notes/Hoch%20Cohomology.pdf"} Graduate student seminar: Hochschild cohomology
          %p In fact it was Gerstenhaber who coined the term ‘pre-Lie algebra’, for this reason.
          %h2#relation_to_the_work_of_connes_and_kreimer Relation to the work of Connes and Kreimer
          %p
            Connes and Kreimer formalized the process of
            %a{:href => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/show/renormalization#hopfalgebraic_renormalization_3"} renormalization
            using a certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hopf+algebra"} Hopf algebra
            built from
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Feynman+diagram"} Feynman diagram
          %ul
            %li
              John Baez, This Week’s Finds in Mathematical Physics, Week 299. (
              %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/week299.html"}> web
              ) (
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/06/this_weeks_finds_in_mathematic_60.html"}> blog
              )
          %p The key is to form the free pre-Lie algebra on one generator, then turn this into a Lie algebra as described above, and then form the universal enveloping of that, which is a cocommutative Hopf algebra. Finally, the restricted dual of this cocommutative Hopf algebra is the commutative Hopf algebra considered by Connes and Kreimer here:
          %ul
            %li
              Alain Connes and Dirk Kreimer, Hopf algebras, renormalization and noncommutative geometry,
              %em Commun. Math. Phys.
              %strong 199
              (1998), 203–242 (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9808042"}> arXiv
              )
          %p
            Pre-Lie algebras are algebras of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+operad"} linear operad
            called
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi PL
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PL
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi PL
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PL_n
            has a basis given by labelled rooted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trees"} trees
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            vertices, and the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi i
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s \circ_i t
            is given by summing all the possible ways of inserting the tree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi t
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} t
            inside the tree
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi s
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
            at the vertex labelled
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
          %ul
            %li
              Frédéric Chapoton, Muriel Livernet, Pre-Lie algebras and the rooted trees operad,
              %em Int. Math. Res. Not.
              2001 (2001), 395-408.
          %p The free pre-Lie algebra on one generator is thus
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi PL
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_{n} PL_n /S_n  \,
          %p
            so the description of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi PL
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} PL_n
            in terms of rooted trees gives a kind of ‘explanation’ of the relation between the Connes–Kreimer Hopf algebra and rooted trees.
          %h2#selfreferentiality Self-referentiality
          %p Pre-Lie algebras have a strange self-referential feature. Every operad of a large class gives a pre-Lie algebra, but the operad for pre-Lie algebras is one of this class! This raises the following interesting puzzle.
          %p
            As we have seen above, for any linear operad
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi O
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} O
            %em is
            a pre-Lie algebra! Do these pre-Lie structures agree?
          %p
            The answer is
            %em no.
            For an explanation, see page 7 here:
          %ul
            %li
              Dominique Manchon, A short survey on pre-Lie algebras. (
              %a{:href => "http://math.univ-bpclermont.fr/~manchon/biblio/ESI-prelie2009.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %h2#references References
          %p The best overall introduction to pre-Lie algebras seems to be that by Dominique Manchon, cited above. For two more introductions, try the following:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                John Baez, This Week’s Finds in Mathematical Physics, Week 299. (
                %a{:href => "http://math.ucr.edu/home/baez/week299.html"}> web
                ) (
                %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2010/06/this_weeks_finds_in_mathematic_60.html"}> blog
                )
            %li
              %p
                Frédéric Chapoton, Operadic point of view on the Hopf algebra of rooted trees. (
                %a{:href => "http://www-math.unice.fr/~patras/CargeseConference/ACQFT09_FredericCHAPOTON.pdf"}> pdf
                )
        .revisedby
